                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ea0d5034-20b9-307c-9ed7-68d09244954c",
                                        "entry_id": "641edb09-c58f-309e-8fa7-a32a60223f1b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The break in the working week, usually two days including the traditional holy or sabbath day. Thus in Western countries, Saturday and Sunday."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1c288d60-6465-43f8-9eae-de38c8f15990",
                                                "sense_id": "ea0d5034-20b9-307c-9ed7-68d09244954c",
                                                "text": "“They can live upon barley-meal without a morsel of meat from week-end to week-end, can these miserable Sawnies,” quoth another."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5aefa9f4-adee-4911-b0a7-f580dfca7edb",
                                                "sense_id": "ea0d5034-20b9-307c-9ed7-68d09244954c",
                                                "text": "[…] often took a few boys down there for what we North Country folk call the week-end — Saturday and Sunday; it was also used as a sanatorium if required."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0074a7a9-ebaf-4390-a026-a0f1f9d93d27",
                                                "sense_id": "ea0d5034-20b9-307c-9ed7-68d09244954c",
                                                "text": "I love a phrase of Dizzy's in one of his later letters to Lady Bradford, whom he reproaches for her addiction to what we now call week-end visits to country houses: “the monotony of organized platitude.”"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
